### F. Local user activity (broader than the configured project)

Goal: spot context that explains the day without limiting scope to the one project. Be conservative — these scans should take seconds, not minutes. Truncate aggressively (top 10–20 per section). NEVER include this data verbatim in the report; summarize.

**F.1 Git activity across ALL repos** — what was user committing/touching outside their main project today:
```bash
# Find every git repo under ~ touched in last 24h, list its today commits
find "$HOME" -maxdepth 4 -name .git -type d -prune -newer ~/.last_meta_run.marker 2>/dev/null \
  | head -30 \
  | while read gitdir; do
      repo="$(dirname "$gitdir")"
      base="$(basename "$repo")"
      commits=$(git -C "$repo" log --author="$(git -C "$repo" config user.email)" --since='today 00:00' --oneline 2>/dev/null | head -10)
      [ -n "$commits" ] && printf '%s:\n%s\n\n' "$base" "$commits"
    done
touch ~/.last_meta_run.marker
```

**F.2 Recent files (VSCode workspace history + macOS mdfind + Downloads)**:
```bash
# VSCode: workspace history index (JSONs naming recent files)
ls -lt "$HOME/Library/Application Support/Code/User/History" 2>/dev/null | head -20

# macOS: files modified today, NOT under common noise dirs
mdfind -onlyin "$HOME" "kMDItemContentModificationDate >= \$time.today" 2>/dev/null \
  | grep -v -E '/Library/|/node_modules/|/\.git/|/\.cache/|/Trash/' \
  | head -30

# Recent downloads
ls -lt "$HOME/Downloads" 2>/dev/null | head -10
```

**F.3 Shell history (with mandatory secret scrub)** — what user ran in terminal today:
```bash
# Tail today's history with timestamp filter; scrub credential-shaped strings
HIST="${HISTFILE:-$HOME/.zsh_history}"
[ -f "$HIST" ] || HIST="$HOME/.bash_history"
[ -f "$HIST" ] && tail -n 500 "$HIST" 2>/dev/null \
  | sed -E \
      -e 's/ghp_[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,}/ghp_<REDACTED>/g' \
      -e 's/ghs_[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,}/ghs_<REDACTED>/g' \
      -e 's/github_pat_[A-Za-z0-9_-]+/github_pat_<REDACTED>/g' \
      -e 's/xox[baprs]-[A-Za-z0-9-]{10,}/xox?-<REDACTED>/g' \
      -e 's/AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}/AKIA<REDACTED>/g' \
      -e 's/sk-ant-[A-Za-z0-9_-]+/sk-ant-<REDACTED>/g' \
      -e 's/Bearer [A-Za-z0-9._-]{20,}/Bearer <REDACTED>/g' \
      -e 's/[A-Za-z0-9._-]{40,}/<REDACTED-LONGSTRING>/g'
```

If a `<REDACTED>` marker appears in the scrubbed output, treat THAT line as fully sensitive — don't quote it verbatim in the report, just say "user ran a curl/echo with a credential present at line N" so the night agent knows to skip the area.

