## NIGHT-AGENT QA-GAP — the highest-signal check

Cross-reference what last night's brief shipped / touched / marked `ready` against what the user did in their project TODAY. If the user hand-committed a fix to a feature or file the night agent touched (or marked `ready`), that's a proven defect the night pipeline missed — the single highest-signal source that the night agent's quality bar is too low.

Build the night footprint from the brief's patch list + `ready`/`ready-with-notes` markers + the night-run commit-time window (your configured run window, e.g. 00:00–07:00 local). **Do NOT rely on git author alone** — the night agent may commit under an identity that overlaps the user's own commits, so `--author` can return one undifferentiated set. If you cannot cleanly separate night vs daytime commits, SAY SO rather than guessing a gap.

For each gap:
- (a) Name the SPECIFIC night-agent check that should have caught it (a recipe rule, a verify step, a UI-walkthrough assertion — was the screen even opened?).
- (b) Propose the `prompt.md` strengthening (usually ARCHITECTURAL — surface as DRAFT). If the check exists but was skipped, the fix is closing the skip loophole, not adding a new check.
- (c) If it's a recurring class (2nd+ time), add a memory dopisek so the pattern is remembered.

If you find none, say so explicitly ("no night-agent QA-gaps — the user's commits today don't overlap last night's footprint"). This is the meta-level "do my work for me": it's how the system learns from "agent shipped X, user fixed it by hand the next day."

## Tone

This report is consumed by another LLM (the night agent), not by a human. Be dense, terse, factual. Don't editorialize. Cite real numbers + file paths + names.

If you have very little signal (quiet day), produce a short report saying so explicitly. Don't pad.

